2026-08-27T00:00:00Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Judging matte quality
 # uuid: a90fad21-c269-5d4c-b566-f739cedf7ddd
 # tags: rfd:title, rfd:attest-in:none
 RFD:Published  1 RFD
 Serials:Weftspun

2026-08-27T00:00:01Z (RFD66606-1153) 'matte evaluation
 # tags: rfd:feature, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:02Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Matte judging uses composites, native crops and two scored categories.
 # tags: rfd:hook
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:03Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Background removal produced three candidate mattes per image and no way to say which was broken.
 # tags: rfd:intrigue
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:04Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Judge a matte as a composite onto a flat backing, in native 100x100 crops, scored for connectivity and gradient apart; the sections below derive where EditScore fits.
 # tags: rfd:exit
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:05Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Judging matte quality
 # tags: rfd:details-title, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:06Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Judging follows the field's standard benchmark. Rhemann et al., *A Perceptually Motivated Online Benchmark for Image Matting* (CVPR 2009), derived error functions from a 17-participant user study because SAD and MSE do not track human judgement. Three of their decisions are adopted, each explaining one failure:
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-1153) '1. The judged artefact is a **composite onto a flat backing**, never raw alpha. Humans were never shown alpha, which is why the twin panel did worse.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-1153) '2. Crops of **100x100 pixels at native resolution**, where alpha is uncertain. Whole downscaled photographs resolved nothing: edge error is invisible at 448px.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-1153) '3. Two categories scored separately: **connectivity** (detached fragments, holes) and **gradient** (oversmoothing, or false hard edges).
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-1153) 'RFD66606-1152 selects the background remover this judges. RFD66606-1006 covers layer decomposition.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Where EditScore fits
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-1153) 'EditScore, configured as published, reproduces the ground-truth ordering of the three backends; misconfigured it inverts it. It cannot pick between them on one image, because it scores *edits* and three mattes of a photograph are near-identical as edits. Use it where candidates differ semantically, and alpha error where they differ by pixels of silhouette.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Result
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-1153) '72 crops, four per cutout, across three backends and six photographs chosen for sheer fabric, specular surfaces and wispy hair.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-1153) '| category | mean | below 6.0 |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-1153) '|---|---|---|
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-1153) '| connectivity | 9.63 | **0 / 72** |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-1153) '| gradient | 3.79 | **47 / 72** |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-1153) 'The failure mode of these background removers on this corpus is **edge fidelity, and only edge fidelity**. Not one crop showed a connectivity fault: no floating fragments, no holes punched through solid regions. 65% of crops showed a gradient fault, an edge either smeared into a blur or, more often here, hardened into an abrupt cut where the real edge is soft.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Unlike the two whole-image formats, this produces variance: connectivity spans 8.0 to 10.0 and gradient spans 0.8 to 8.0.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Crop selection
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-1153) 'The study selected crops by hand for exhibiting one artefact category. Automating that: artefacts live where the matte is uncertain, so candidate windows are ranked by how much partial alpha they contain, taken at native resolution and spaced apart so k crops are not k views of one artefact.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Measured concentration on a wispy-hair source: whole-image soft alpha 3.97%, selected crops 35-75%. A 9 to 19 times enrichment, which is the mechanism that puts artefacts inside the judge's input budget.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Configuration, not capability
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-1153) 'EditScore's stated applications are best-of-N reranking and supplying an RL reward signal. Both failed on first attempt here, and the cause was how it was configured rather than the model.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Three faults, all ours:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-1153) '* `num_pass=1` at temperature 0.7, a single stochastic sample. Self-ensembling is how the paper reaches its headline numbers.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-1153) '* a pairwise "is B better than A" comparator, which is not how best-of-N works: candidates are scored independently against the source and the best wins. That comparator was position-driven in 37 of 52 pairings.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-1153) '* custom single-image rubrics, which bypass the SC pass entirely and are not EditScore at all.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:30Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Corrected, published SC rubric, source and candidate as the pair, an edit instruction, `num_pass=3`, judged on 100px crops, it reproduces the ground-truth ordering on the alphamatting.com set:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:31Z (RFD66606-1153) '| backend | overall | following | true SAD |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:32Z (RFD66606-1153) '|---|---:|---:|---:|
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:33Z (RFD66606-1153) '| birefnet-hr-matting | 4.07 | 5.39 | 4.00 |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:34Z (RFD66606-1153) '| birefnet-matting | 3.81 | 4.97 | 4.90 |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:35Z (RFD66606-1153) '| birefnet | 3.44 | 4.78 | 8.15 |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:36Z (RFD66606-1153) '48 judgements over 8 images. Both score columns descend monotonically as true error rises, and the ordering held when the sample was tripled from an earlier 15-judgement run. The misconfigured run had ranked the segmenter *best* on gradient, which ground truth inverts.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:37Z (RFD66606-1153) '**Aggregate ranking works; per-sample selection does not.** Spearman against true SAD is -0.237, right sign, strengthening with sample size (-0.139 at n=15), still weak. Best-of-N picked the true best on 6 of 16 crops, 37.5% against a 33% chance rate: at n=15 it read 3 of 5 and that was noise.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:38Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Why per-sample fails, from the methodology
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:39Z (RFD66606-1153) 'The split is not a defect in the model. It follows from what EditScore is built to score.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:40Z (RFD66606-1153) 'EditScore scores *an edit*: a source image, an edited result, and the instruction that was supposed to have been applied. Its SC pass asks whether the instruction was followed and whether the result stays consistent with the source. Best-of-N, in the repository's own usage, means N generations of the same edit from the same model differing by seed, candidates that differ in visible, semantic content.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:41Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Our candidates are three mattes of one photograph. As whole images they are nearly identical: they differ by a few pixels of alpha along one silhouette. When the judge returned the same score for multiple backends on 9 of 16 comparisons, that was not a failure to discriminate. It was an accurate report that the images are near-identical *as edits*. The question it answers is correct; it is not the question a matte comparison asks.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:42Z (RFD66606-1153) 'The same fact explains why the aggregate ordering survives. A small consistent bias toward the better matte averages up over sixteen judgements. On any single comparison it sits below the model's resolution, and below the 0.4 quantisation step that `score_range=25` implies, only 24 distinct `overall` values appeared across 48 judgements.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:43Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Consequence
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:44Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Use EditScore where the candidates differ semantically at whole-image scale: whether an edit happened, whether the subject was altered, whether residue remains. Do not use it to grade edge quality between near-identical candidates.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:45Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Alpha error, SAD, MSE, gradient, connectivity against ground truth, is the field's instrument for that, and it settled the model choice unambiguously in a single run where the judge needed 48 to reach a weak correlation. RFD66606-1152 records that result.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:46Z (RFD66606-1153) 'What this does not establish
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:47Z (RFD66606-1153) '**The between-backend ordering**, for the reasons above. Model selection was settled separately, against ground truth: see RFD66606-1152.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:48Z (RFD66606-1153) 'No rationales
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:49Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Every fault carries `reasoning: "concise"`. The Apple-silicon backbone prefills the assistant turn as far as `{"reasoning": "concise", "score": [` so that only the score tokens are generated, which is roughly 20 times less decoding. The reasoning field is therefore a constant supplied by us, not model output.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:50Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Scores are unaffected. Recovering explanations means dropping the prefill and paying full decode.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:51Z (RFD66606-1153) 'Rubric scale hazard
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:52Z (RFD66606-1153) 'EditScore builds its rubrics as `text.replace('10', str(score_range))` and divides the answer by `score_range / 10`. A rubric injected without the same replace is answered on a 0-10 scale and rescaled as if it were 0-25. Full marks then read 4.00, and the range looks compressed into three levels when it is not.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:53Z (RFD66606-1153) 'The problem
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:54Z (RFD66606-1153) 'The available proxy, soft-alpha pixels per silhouette perimeter, ranks model families but cannot judge one matte. Two attempts with EditScore failed: whole images flattened onto grey scored near-constant within each image, so the judge was reading the photograph rather than the cutout, and a twin panel of cutout beside alpha returned exactly 8.00 for all eighteen.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1153  1 LINE
 Text:Written
